// The zstd package: Zstandard (RFC 8878) decompression, with its xxHash64,
// streaming in bounded pieces and in one call. Written in Cone, with no C
// library and no call of the OS's. Compression is not here yet.
//
//   mod unpack;
//   import stdio use *;
//   import zstd;
//
//   fn main() i32 {
//     imm packed = ...;                                  // a .zst file's bytes
//     match zstd.decompress(packed.view(), 0usize) {     // every frame, joined
//       case imm ok Ok { printInt(i64.from(ok.len())); }
//       case imm e Error { printStr(e.reason.view()); return 1i32; }
//     }
//     0i32;
//   }
//
// It reads as package deflate does: a value that holds a stream's whole
// state, fed and pulled in bounded pieces, and one-call helpers beside it.
//
// WHAT IS HERE
//
//   ONE CALL         the data in memory, the answer out
//     decompress(data, expected) Result[List[u8], ZstdError]
//     decompressWithDictionary(data, dictionary, expected) Result[List[u8], ZstdError]
//   'expected' is the output size if known (0 if not): it reserves room and is
//   also the most output allowed (0 allows 1 GiB), so a stream that claims to be
//   small and is not is refused as TooLarge. Every frame in 'data' is decoded
//   and their contents joined, skippable frames skipped, as 'zstd -d' does; no
//   input at all is no frames, an empty output.
//
//   STREAMING        a value that holds the whole state; any chunking
//     Decompressor.make()
//       feed(&Array[u8])          input, any size, split anywhere (copied)
//       finish()                  no more input will come
//       pull(&mut List[u8], max) Status    appends at most 'max' bytes of output
//       setLimit(bytes), setMaxWindow(bytes), setDictionary(&content),
//       setMultiFrame(bool), error(), frameCount(), totalIn(), totalOut(),
//       isDone(), takeRest()
//     Status: NeedInput (use up what you have: feed more or finish), MoreOutput
//     (pull again), Done, Failed ('error()' says why).
//     'examples/tour.cone' feeds five bytes at a time and takes eight out.
//
//   CHECKSUM
//     xxh64(data, seed) u64, Xxh64 (make(seed), update(data, from, to),
//     digest()) to go on across pieces. A frame's content checksum is the low
//     32 bits of xxh64(content, 0).
//
//   TYPES
//     ZstdError {kind, offset, reason}   kind is an ErrorKind: BadHeader,
//         WindowTooLarge, NeedsDictionary, BadBlock, BadTable, BadSequence,
//         BadOffset, Truncated, BadChecksum, TooLarge; 'offset' is the input
//         bytes used before the unit that failed (a frame header, a block, a
//         checksum)
//
// WHAT IS DECODED: all of RFC 8878's frame format. Frames: the magic number,
// the frame header (window descriptor, dictionary ID, content size in 1, 2, 4
// or 8 bytes, single segment), the content checksum; skippable frames (all 16
// magic numbers); any number of frames in a row. Blocks: raw, RLE and
// compressed. Literals: raw and RLE (header of 1, 2 or 3 bytes), Huffman-coded
// in 1 or 4 streams (every size format), the tree by direct 4-bit weights or
// FSE-coded weights, and "treeless" (the frame's last tree again). Sequences:
// the 1-, 2- and 3-byte counts; for each of literal lengths, offsets and match
// lengths the predefined, RLE, FSE-coded and repeat modes; the three repeat
// offsets, as the RFC moves them (a literal length of 0 shifting them by one).
// The window, and a raw-content dictionary.
//
// STREAMING, IN A SENTENCE EACH
//
//   A Decompressor decodes one unit at a time: a magic number, a frame
//   header, a block with its 3-byte header (at most 128 KiB), a checksum. A
//   unit is decoded only once all of its bytes are in, so input can be cut
//   anywhere, a byte at a time, and nothing is half-done. A block is decoded
//   whole, into the history, and handed out in pieces of at most 'max' bytes;
//   the next block is decoded only when that one has all been handed out. A
//   frame may be followed by another, so the stream is Done only after
//   'finish' (or, with 'setMultiFrame(false)', at the first frame's end). The
//   Decompressor's own memory is the input not yet used, the window (all of
//   the frame's output while it is no longer than the window), one block, and
//   as much again as the window before it lets the old output go.
//
// METHOD CHOICES. These are defaults a later design may revisit. Each says what
// was chosen and what was left out.
//
// - Decoding is RFC 8878's, as the reference decoder (zstd's
//   lib/decompress) does it, without its speed tricks: an FSE table of
//   (symbol, bits, base) per state; a Huffman table indexed by the next
//   maxBits bits that answers one symbol per look; a backward bit reader that
//   reads zeros past the start of its bytes and counts them. Every bitstream
//   must decode to exactly its end, as the reference requires: bits left over,
//   or more read than there are, fail the block. The Huffman streams are
//   decoded one after another (the reference decodes the four at once,
//   interleaved, two symbols a look); the sequences are executed as they are
//   decoded; every copy is a byte at a time.
// - Streaming by whole units, not by a state machine that can stop at any
//   bit. The cost: a block's bytes are kept until all of them have come (at
//   most 128 KiB), and decoded at once. The gain: one decoder for one-call and
//   streamed use, and a hot loop with no stops in it.
// - The window: the largest one a frame may ask for is 128 MiB at first
//   (setMaxWindow), as the zstd tool's default (ZSTD_WINDOWLOG_LIMIT_DEFAULT,
//   2^27); a frame asking for more is refused with WindowTooLarge before
//   anything of it is decoded. The one-call helpers do not apply it: they keep
//   the whole output anyway, so a window costs them nothing (the reference's
//   one-call ZSTD_decompress does not apply it either); their bound is the
//   output limit. A single-segment frame's window is its content size.
// - A match may reach back as far as the window (RFC 8878 section 3.1.1.1.2),
//   and no further: an offset past it is BadOffset. (The reference decoder is
//   more lenient: it takes any offset within the output it holds; the tests
//   show it accepting one 1 byte past the window.) With a dictionary, matches
//   may reach into it while the frame's output is no longer than the window,
//   as the reference compressor uses a dictionary (it stops when a block ends
//   past the window).
// - A block decodes to at most the block maximum (the window, at most 128
//   KiB), compressed or not, as the reference requires.
// - A Huffman tree's codes are at most 11 bits (RFC 8878 section 4.2.1; the
//   reference allows 12), and, as the reference requires, it has at least two
//   codes of the longest length.
// - A bomb stopper: output is capped at 1 GiB unless 'setLimit' (or the
//   one-call 'expected') says otherwise, as deflate's is; a frame header that
//   declares more is refused at once. The room reserved up front is never
//   more than 64 MiB on a header's word.
// - Dictionaries: raw content only (zstd's "rawContent" mode: the bytes are
//   taken as content that comes before each frame, the repeat offsets start at
//   1, 4 and 8). A frame that names a dictionary by ID is refused with
//   NeedsDictionary, with or without a dictionary given (a raw-content
//   dictionary has no ID to match, as the reference refuses it). zstd's own
//   dictionary format (magic EC30A437, entropy tables and repeat offsets) can
//   follow.
// - Empty input is no frames and decodes to nothing (as ZSTD_decompress); a
//   frame cut anywhere is Truncated; bytes after a frame that are not another
//   frame are BadHeader.
// - xxHash64 is here, not in cryptohash: it is not a cryptographic hash, zstd
//   is its only user, and this lane adds one package and touches no other.
//   It takes 32 bytes a step in four lanes (Yann Collet's XXH64).
// - The tables of RFC 8878 (literal and match length codes, the three
//   predefined distributions) are immutable module-level arrays (tables.cone),
//   as deflate's RFC 1951 tables are. Everything else is in the values that
//   use it: nothing is shared between two Decompressors, so one per thread or
//   actor is safe; one is not safe to share.
// - The block decoder takes the input as '&List[u8]', not as a slice: the
//   borrow checker (error 1339) refuses a slice of one field of the
//   Decompressor alongside '&mut' to the others, which change shape, as it
//   cannot tell the fields apart; a '&List[u8]' lends no elements across the
//   call.
//
// TESTS. Made by the reference, libzstd 1.5.7 as Git for Windows installs it
// (C:\Program Files\Git\mingw64\bin\libzstd.dll, SHA-256
// b95c223a9548a9ecf51377c962e0bc8f0c51eb0c6f67a296dbc885996f0dd40d), called
// through Python's ctypes only to MAKE test data (tests/data/zstdref.py pins
// it; nothing runs it at test time): tests/data/make_corpus.py writes
// corpus.bin (levels 1, 3, 9, 19 and 22, long-distance mode, streamed frames,
// every block, literal and sequence mode, tiny and empty inputs, many frames,
// a dictionary), and make_handmade.py writes handmade.bin and malformed.bin
// with its own small encoder, each frame checked against the reference. The
// tests decode every frame in one call and streamed every way, refuse every
// malformed one with the right kind split at every offset, flip every bit of
// some frames, and check xxHash64 against the reference's own.
//
// OTHER COPIES. The repository was searched (Cone sources in packages/,
// samples/, tools/, compiler/) for another zstd, FSE, Huffman-literal or
// xxHash decoder: there is none.
//
// NOT HERE: compression (the next pull request: levels 1 to 3); zstd's own
// dictionary format and dictionary training; the legacy formats before
// RFC 8878 (zstd 0.x); reading from a file or a socket (the caller feeds it);
// multithreaded decoding.
//
// SPEED, best of 5 runs on a loaded 13th-gen Core i7-13700HX, release build,
// in MB/s of the decompressed bytes, next to the reference library
// (libzstd 1.5.7, Git for Windows' build, called once per file through
// Python's ctypes) on the same files, made by the reference at levels 3 and
// 19 (examples/bench.cone makes the first columns):
//
//                                  one call    streamed    reference
//   Cone's packages' sources L3      257         218          526
//     (17.7 MB)              L19     308         237          540
//   conec.exe, first 16 MB   L3      181         160          508
//                            L19     175         140          303
//   pbrxrt.spv (385 KB)      L3      212         154          385
//                            L19     114          87          279
//
// So it runs at 35 to 60 per cent of the reference's speed. The likely cause
// is the copying: every literal and every match byte is moved one at a time
// through a List (the reference copies 16 or 32 bytes a step, overlapping
// past the end), and a streamed pull moves each byte once more. Then the
// Huffman streams decoded one at a time, one symbol a look, and the bit reader
// refilled a byte at a time. These are measured, not chased: nothing here has
// been tuned for speed past the shape of the algorithms.
// XXH64 runs at 6.7 GB/s.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   zstd.cone          this overview
//   types.cone         Status, ErrorKind, ZstdError
//   tables.cone        the constants of RFC 8878
//   xxhash.cone        xxh64, Xxh64
//   fse.cone           the bit readers, FSE table descriptions and tables
//   huffman.cone       Huffman tree descriptions, tables and streams
//   block.cone         a compressed block: literals, sequences, their tables
//   decompressor.cone  Decompressor: frames, blocks, the window, streaming
//   decompress.cone    decompress, decompressWithDictionary

mod zstd;

import collections use *;
